Port / Service enumeration tools:
==================================
=> Ports are logical endpoints used for communication between devices on a network. They allow multiple services to run on the same machine by assigning each service a unique identifier.
=> Port numbers: Range from 0-65535, divided into:
        -> 0-1023: Well-known ports - Standardized for specific services (eg: HTTP on port 80, SSH on port 22)
        -> 1024-49151: Registered ports - For user applications or custom services.
        -> 49152-65535: Dynamic/Private ports - Temporary ports for client-side communication.
Example: When visiting example.com, your browser connects to port 80 (HTTP) or 443 (HTTPS).
=> Ports help in:
        -> Service discovery - identifying open ports reveals available services (eg: SSH, RDP, HTTP)
        -> Attack surface expansion - Each service represents a potential entry point for attacks (eg: brute-forcing SSH or exploiting vulnerable web servers)

Port discovery tools:
--------------------
=> Nmap:
-------
-> Nmap (Network Mapper) - open source tool for network discovery and security auditing, designed to scan large networks efficiently and identify open ports, services, and vulnerabilities.

Key features:-
-> Port scanning - identifies open ports on target systems
-> Service detection - Determines services running on open ports (eg: HTTP, FTP)
-> OS Detection - Attempts to identify the operating system of the target.
-> Scriptable - Uses NSE (Nmap Scripting Engine) for vulnerability detection and automation

Basic Nmap scan:-
-> nmap 192.168.1.1
        => It's called a "CONNECT" scan. Testing only the 1000 most-used TCP ports.

Running a "SYN" scan:-
-> The normal "CONNECT" scan uses the full 3-way handshake to test the port. A port is marked as "open" when the 3-way handshake is successful, and Nmap receives a "SYN-ACK."
-> The normal "CONNECT" can be easily identified and is blocked by some firewalls. A variant that is a little bit more stealthy and faster is the "SYN" scan.
-> We call this a "half-open scan" because the 3-way handshake does not complete.
-> To run the test, a client sends a SYN to start the 3-way handshake.
-> When the target responds with "SYN-ACK" something is listening, and the port is marked as "open". After this, the connection is closed, and the 3-way handshake is not completed.
-> To use this option Nmap needs to use "Raw Sockets" which requires "root" privileges.
-> When Nmap is run as root a SYN scan is the default scan!
              $  sudo nmap 192.168.1.1

Services usually found these known ports:-
21 - FTP, 22 - SSH, 25 - SMTP, 88 - Kerberos, 161/162 - SNMP, 389/636 - LDAP(S), 443 - HTTPS, 445 - SMB, 3306 - MySQL, 3389 - RDP, 5900 - VNC

Nmap variants besides "CONNECT" and "SYN":-
=> -sT: Default scan (when not using sudo). The "CONNECT" scan, which works by performing a full 3-way handshake.
=> -sS: Default scan (when using sudo), the TCP-SYN scan / Half-Open scan.
=> -sA: TCP-ACK scan. Sends only an ACK packet to the target port in the hope of receiving an RST in return (used for firewall testing).
=> -sX: XMAS scan. This scan is very noisy and tests all TCP flags (FIN/ PSH/ URG). 
=> -sU: UDP scan. This scan also requires "raw sockets" and needs to be run with sudo privileges
                sudo nmap -sU 192.168.1.1

Chaining IP addresses and ports:-
=> Use commas to separate individual values
        sudo nmap 192.168.1.1, 192.168.1.2, 192.168.1.3
=> Use a dash (-) to specify a range (eg: from 1 to 4)
        sudo nmap 192.168.1.1-3
=> Use a slash (/) after an IP address to define a subnet range
        sudo nmap 192.168.1.1/24

Important arguments of Nmap:-
=> -h: help
=> -v: Verbose mode (use -vv or -vvv for increased verbosity)
=> -sV: Enumerate versions (banner grabbing)
=> -sC: Script scan. Use the default scripts on open ports to identify vulnerabilities and gather other interesting information.
=> -p: Specify port(s). -p- scans all 65535 ports 
=> -Pn: No-Ping scan. Skips the ping step to determine if the host is live.
=> -O: OS detection. Identifies the target OS
=> -sL: List scan (ping scan). Lists available hosts.
=> -oA: Outputs the results in all supported formats (-oG = Greppable | -oN = Nmap | -oX = XML)
=> -iL: Input List. Specify an input list containing multiple hosts to scan.

Some examples:-
=> Scan all 65535 TCP ports of 2 hosts with a SYN scan:
        sudo nmap -p- 192.168.1.1, 192.168.1.2
=> Scan all 65535 UDP ports of 2 hosts:
        sudo -sU nmap -p- 192.168.1.1-2
=> Scan all 65535 TCP ports with a CONNECT scan and retrieve the hosts to scan from an input list and output all the results:
        nmap -p- -iL ~/Desktop/hosts.txt -oA ~/Desktop/nmap-results.txt
=> Most used scan:
        sudo nmap -sC -sV -p- 192.168.1.1 -oA ~/Desktop/nmap-192-168-1-1

SYN scan - Script scan - Version scan - All ports
For stealth, we can first scan live hosts and then perform a SYN scan, and we disable pinging all the devices first. Scan only the 100 most used ports:
        nmap -sL 192.168.1.1/24
        sudo nmap -sS -Pn -F -iL ~/Desktop/alive-hosts.txt

Note: When evading a blue team, consider using the CONNECT scan (-sT) because this blends in with normal traffic
Nmap's "top ports" are specified in the /usr/share/nmap/nmap-services file. The third column in this file represents the frequency at which these ports are found to be open. We can modify Nmap's top ports by adjusting this value.

When you need a fast overview of all web ports, perform the following:-
1) Perform a web scan and output at least the XML
        sudo nmap -p 80,443,4443,8000,8080,8443 192.168.1.1 -oA web-results
2) Install the Google Chrome or Chromium browser
3) Download the latest release of Aquatone:
        https://github.com/michenriksen/aquatone/releases/tag/v1.7.0
4) Run the tool to screenshot the nmap results:
        cat ~/Desktop/web-results.xml | ./aquatone -nmap -out capture
5) Check the resulting screenshots in the Aquatone folder (/capture/screenshots)

Another Screenshot tool is Eyewitness: (https://github.com/RedSiege/EyeWitness)
1) Download Eyewitness:
        git clone https://github.com/RedSiege/EyeWitness.git 
2) Run setup.sh script in the "/Python/setup" directory of EyeWitness:
        pip3 install –r requirements.txt
        sudo./setup.sh
3) Now run the tool on the Nmap output:
        ./EyeWitness.py -x ~/Desktop/web-results.xml --web -d ./eyewitness_report

Nmap NSE Scripts: 
-> The Nmap Scripting Engine (NSE) allows Nmap to go beyond port scanning by performing advanced tasks using customizable scripts.
-> Written in Lua, these scripts can automate a wide variety of network reconnaissance and vulnerability assessment tasks. Script location: /usr/share/nmap/scripts
Key features:
• Detect vulnerabilities, misconfigurations, and weak credentials.
• Gather additional target information, such as service banners or detailed software versions.
• Perform brute force attacks, exploit tests, or even malware detection.
• NSE scripts are organized into categories, such as: discovery (e.g., dns-brute, ssh
hostkey), vulnerability (e.g., http-vuln-cve2017-5638), brute (e.g., ftp-brute, ssh-brute) 
and exploit (e.g., ms17-010).

Examples:
=> Scan with a specific NSE script:
        nmap--script=http-title 192.168.1.1
=> Scan with 2 NSE scripts:
        nmap --script=http-title,dns-zone-transfer 192.168.1.1
=> Scan based on category (all SMB scripts):
        nmap--script=smb-* 192.168.1.1
=> Pass additional parameters to scripts using the --script-args option.
        nmap--script=ftp-anon --script-args anonmax=10 192.168.1.1

Nmap Alternatives:
-------------------
• Alternatives to Nmap include MASSCAN and RustScan.
• These alternatives are faster because they send much more simultaneous traffic. However, they also generate more traffic and are noisier than Nmap!
• When we are on a Windows host, we can also scan for open ports. For example, we can use PowerShell’s "Test-NetConnection":
        Test-NetConnection-Port 445 192.168.1.1
• We can also use PowerShell scripting to create a fully functional PowerShell port scanner:
1..1024 | % {echo ((New-Object Net.Sockets.TcpClient).Connect("192.168.1.1", $_)) "TCP port $_ is open"} 2>$null

=> NetCat:
-----------
• NetCatis a command-line network tool used for network connectivity and data transfers.
• NetCatprovides the ability to create TCP and UDP connections, and also supports other protocols such 
as HTTP, SSL, and more.
• NetCat can be used for testing network connections, sending and receiving data, and performing 
network diagnostics.
• NetCatoffers a wide range of options and functionalities, including port scanning, port forwarding, 
banner grabbing, and redirecting network traffic.
• NetCatis frequently used by security researchers and penetration testers to identify and exploit 
vulnerabilities in networks and applications.
• NetCat(netcat / nc) and NCat (ncat) are two network tools that have many similarities. We work with 
the default NetCat library. NCat adds extra features on top of NetCat's functionality, such as support for 
SSL encryption, proxy connections, banner grabbing, IPv6, and more. NCat is developed by the same 
creators of Nmap and is therefore also part of the Nmap suite.

Important Arguments:-
-h: Help
-v: Verbose mode: Provides detailed output on NetCat's activities. Used for debugging (vv for extra verbosity)
-l: Listen mode. This allows NetCat to listen for incoming connections on a specified port.
-p: Port specification: Allows you to specify the port to listen to or connect to.
-k: Keep connection open: This flag keeps the connection open after input ends (interactive connection)
-e: Execute program: This allows you to run a program on the host when a connection is made.
-u: UDP mode: Switches to UDP mode for sending and receiving UDP packets instead of TCP.
-c: Command-line: Allows you to give NetCat commands via the command line.
-z: Zero I/O mode: Sends no data but only establishes the connection. This flag is used for port scanning.
-n: NoDNS: Do not attempt to resolve hostnames to IP addresses.
-w: Timeout: Specifies after how many seconds the connection will "time out" and the application will continue.

For executing a portscan, we can use the following NetCat command:
        nc -nvv -w 1 -z 192.168.1.1 3388-3390
nc: NetCat binary
-n: NoDNS
-vv: Double verbose
-w 1: 1 second timeout
-z: Port scanning argument
192.168.1.1: The host
3388-3390: The 3 ports to scan.

Uploading & Downloading Files:
When both the client and the server have NetCat installed, they can exchange files between each other 
(commonly used for data exfiltration).
• The sending side transmits the file.
• The listening side receives the binary data and writes it to a file.

Receiving Side / Listening Side:
-l: Listen mode
-p 8888: Listen on port 8888
> file.txt: Write all received data into file.txt
nc -l -p 8888 > file.txt

Sending Side:
-q: Stop after EOF on stdin and a 5-second delay
10.10.10.10: The IP address of the receiving side
8888: The port number the receiving side is listening on
< data.txt: The file that will be sent
nc -q 5 10.10.10.10 8888 < data.txt

Opening a listener for a Reverse Shell:
nc -lnvp 8888
-l: Listen mode
-n: No DNS
-v: Verbose mode
-p: Port number on which to listen for incoming traffic

=> RPC Specific Tools:
======================
A Remote Procedure Call (RPC) allows a program to execute a procedure (subroutine) on a different machine as if it were a local call.

Key Idea:
RPC abstracts the communication between two systems, making the remote procedure look like a local procedure call. 
RPC is usually language-independent, meaning that client and server software can be written in different programming languages.

Purpose:
RPC enables distributed systems to communicate seamlessly over networks, commonly used in client-server models.

Port assignment:
By default, RPC uses ports in the ephemeral port range (1024-5000) when it assigns ports to RPC applications that have to listen on a TCP endpoint. 
This behavior can make restricting access to these ports challenging for network administrators.

Types of RPC:-
=> XMP-RPC: Uses XML to encode the request and HTTP as a transport mechanism.
=> JSON-RPC: Similar to XML-RPC but uses JSON for encoding and is typically over HTTP or WebSockets.
=> gRPC: Developed by Google, uses Protocol Buffers, faster and more efficient than older RPC systems.
=> DCOM (Distributed Component Object Model): Windows-based RPC mechanism for inter-process communication in distributed applications.

Common Vulnerabilities in RPC:
Retrieving sensitive data: Insufficient input sanitization can lead to remote code execution (RCE).
Buffer Overflow: Malicious input can cause buffer overflows in RPC-based applications, leading to arbitrary code execution.
Authentication Weaknesses: Insecure RPC implementations may allow attackers to bypass authentication, gaining unauthorized access.
Denial of Service (DoS): Attackers can flood RPC services with requests, overwhelming the server.
Man-in-the-Middle (MITM) Attacks: Lack of encryption in RPC can expose sensitive data or allow malicious interception.

rpcclient tool:
Usage: rpcclient is a tool in the Samba suite that can be used to interact with Windows-based RPC services.
Purpose: It can be used to list shares, enumerate users, and access sensitive file system information on Windows systems.

=> Connecting with rpcclient:
        rpcclient -U Administrator%Welcome01 192.168.1.1

=> Connect with domain credentials:
        rpcclient-U DOMAIN/Administrator%Welcome01 192.168.1.1

=> Connect with domain credentials and ask for a password (anonymous):
        rpcclient-U DOMAIN/Administrator -N 192.168.1.1

=> Connect to a specific port:
        rpcclient-U DOMAIN/Administrator%Welcome01 -p 40001 192.168.1.1

rpcclient supports many authentication methods, like Kerberos and NT hashes

Once connected, we receive an RPC shell. Depending on our rights, we can query the system.
=> Domain information: querydominfo
=> Domain users: enumdomusers
=> Specific user enumeration: queryuser <username>
=> Enumerate shares: netshareenumall

=> NetBIOS-specific tools:
===========================
NetBIOS (Network Basic Input/Output System) is a networking protocol that allows applications on different computers to communicate over a local area network (LAN).
Introduced by IBM in 1983, later adapted by Microsoft.
Purpose: Provides services related to name resolution, session establishment, and data transport over a network.

Key Components of NetBIOS:
NetBIOS Name Service (NBNS): Resolves NetBIOS names to IP addresses (like DNS for NetBIOS).
NetBIOS Datagram Service: Provides connectionless communication for broadcasting messages.
NetBIOS Session Service: Establishes and manages reliable, connection-oriented communication between nodes.

NetBIOS often works over port 137 (UDP), 138 (UDP), and 139 (TCP).

Why NetBIOS is Vulnerable:-
Lack of Encryption: Communication via NetBIOS is often unencrypted, making it easy to intercept.
Open Ports: NetBIOS commonly runs on exposed ports (137-139).
Broadcast Nature: NetBIOS datagrams can be broadcast, potentially leaking sensitive information.
Weak Authentication: Older implementations lack robust authentication mechanisms.
Overexposure on the Internet: Misconfigured NetBIOS can expose internal networks to external threats.

NetBIOS Exploitation Tools:
=> Nmap - NetBIOS Enumeration:
Usage: Identify NetBIOS services and open ports.
Purpose: Discovers NetBIOS names and possible vulnerabilities.
Example:
nmap -p 139 --script nbstat <target_ip>

=> NBTScan- NetBIOS Name Scanning:
Usage: Scan for NetBIOS names, MAC addresses, and workgroups.
Purpose: Enumerates NetBIOS names and exposed resources.
Example:
sudo nbtscan -r 192.168.1.1

=> Metasploit - Exploitation Framework:
Usage: Exploit SMB vulnerabilities and misconfigured shares over NetBIOS.
Purpose: Automates attacks like credential harvesting and service enumeration.
Example:
use auxiliary/scanner/netbios/nbname

=> Responder - Man-in-the-Middle Attacks:
Usage: Poison NetBIOS Name Service (NBNS) requests to capture credentials.
Purpose: Intercepts and manipulates NetBIOS traffic.
Example:
sudo responder -I <interface>

=> nbtstat- NetBIOS Diagnostic Tool:
Usage: A built-in Windows tool for diagnosing and enumerating NetBIOS over TCP/IP.
Purpose: Identify active NetBIOS sessions and enumerate workgroups, usernames, and 
potential targets for exploitation.
Example:
nbtstat-A <hostname>
